# Age

Use these terms for stages of life and age groups. Read [Use clear language](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-060) first: do not assume who is reading.

## Stages of life

Use these terms for different stages of life. The ages show what each term relates to.

- fertilised egg: from conception to 14 days
- embryo: from 2 to 6 weeks
- baby: during pregnancy, at birth and up to 1 year
- toddler: 1 to 3 years
- child: 4 to 12 years
- teenager: 13 to 19 years
- young people: 16 to 24 years
- adult: generally from 18 years, but this may vary (see below)

Example: a bedtime routine for children aged 4 to 12 years.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Bedtime scenes for kids | Bedtime routine for children aged 4 to 12 years |

## Writing about specific age groups

Include age only if it is relevant, for example when something applies to a particular age group or when a person needs to be a certain age to do something.

When you need to be specific, give the actual age.

Example: wording for a household rule.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Only grown-ups can change the alarm settings. | Only people aged 18 and over can change the alarm settings. |

### People over or under a specific age

When the exact age matters, do not write “adults over 50” or “teenagers under 18 years old”. This can confuse people who are that exact age. Make it clear who is included.

- Write “adults aged 50 and over”.
- Write “teenagers aged 17 and under”.

Use “over x” or “under x” only in general contexts, or where your source is not specific, for example “this mostly affects people over 60”.

#### “And” or “or”

When you write about groups of people in the third person, use either “aged 18 and over” or “aged 18 or over”.

When you address a person in the second person, use “or”, for example “if you are aged 18 or over”.

### People between 2 ages

Do not use dashes between ages. Write “aged 4 to 16 years”, not “4 – 16 years”. For example:

- children aged 6 months to 4 years
- children aged 12 to 15 years
- adults aged 19 to 64

Read [Ranges of numbers, dates and time](../writing-guidelines/numbers-measurements-dates-time.html#ranges).

### Whether to include “years”

For adults, you can use just the number, or add “years”. For example, “adults aged 18 and over” or “adults aged 18 years and over”.

Keep it brief. “Adults aged 18 years old and over” is too long.

If you write about young children, include “years” to make it clear you are not referring to months.

### Babies and young children

Count the age of babies and young children in weeks up to 6 months, then in months up to 2 years. This is how people talk about a child’s age, and it keeps milestones consistent and accurate.

Example: a baby monitor.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Baby is 0.4 years old | Baby is 21 weeks old |
| Baby is 0.9 years old | Baby is 11 months old |

### Older people

Prefer to specify ages, for example “adults aged 65 and over”.

In some contexts, use “older person” or “older people”, for example where the risk of something increases as people get older.

Example:

Older people are more likely to fall at night. Keep the stairs light on a motion sensor after dark.

Do not use these words:

- elderly
- middle aged
- OAP
- old age pensioner
- pensioner
- senior

Example: a care check-in reminder for an older relative, and heating advice.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Check in on the elderly | Check in with Margaret |
| Heating tips for seniors | Heating tips for adults aged 65 and over |

The names and ages in these examples are made up.
